# Deferred work

- Partial setup failure leaks Vulkan objects: if `Renderer::new` fails after
  the swapchain exists (e.g. a `Frame::new` call fails), the swapchain and any
  already-created frames are never destroyed, and neither are the pieces of a
  half-built `Frame` or `Swapchain`. Fix with the deletion queue, so every
  created object is registered for destruction as soon as it exists.
- Split depth ranges: one near plane can't serve both a cockpit 1 m away and a
  planet 10,000 km away (reverse-Z depth below ~1e-8 fails the depth test).
  Render the far scene with a large near plane, clear depth, then render
  nearby objects with a small one, in the same frame. Needed once vessels
  exist; until then the near plane follows the camera's altitude.
- The triangle pipeline is built for the swapchain's color format. If a
  swapchain recreation ever picks a different format (e.g. the window moves to
  a monitor with other surface formats), the pipeline no longer matches. Goes
  away once scene rendering targets its own fixed-format HDR image.

# Planned features

- Real solar system at scale: bodies generated from real physical and orbital
  parameters. The general shape must be right; fine detail may be procedural.
- Planet textures from the Sol mod (CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0): attribution required,
  no commercial use, derived textures stay under the same license. Keep the
  texture pipeline source-agnostic so they can be swapped for NASA-derived
  data before any commercial release.
- Texture streaming by distance: only bodies near the camera get high
  resolution textures; distant ones keep a small mip level resident.
- Planet LOD: cube-sphere faces split into quadtrees as the camera approaches;
  each tile has its own local origin to keep f32 precision on the surface.
- Seamless map mode: the map is the live world zoomed out. The camera stays
  centered on the current target (no automatic re-targeting); map overlays
  (orbits, markers) fade in only once zoomed out far enough to need them.
- Camera modes:
  - Debug: creative-style fly camera — WASD horizontal, Space/Shift up/down,
    scroll changes speed, mouse captured.
  - Game: locked on a target — hold right click to capture the cursor and
    orbit, scroll zooms in and out.
- Target transition arc (undecided — depends on how the plain transition
  feels): pull the orbit camera further out mid-transition in proportion to
  the distance traveled, so long hops show both bodies in context.
- Time control: pause and warp rates limited by situation (in atmosphere, in
  orbit, eventually interstellar). Rewind is out of scope for performance
  reasons; if ever added, debug mode only.
- Game modes:
  - Survival: career with gradual unlocks and a realistic feel.
  - Creative: survival with infinite resources.
  - Debug: cheats and dev tools — edit positions, orbits and other state.
    The mode used to build the game, also available to players.
